Awesome! 😄 This is one of my favorite features to teach because it introduces another important React concept while making the UI much more professional.

Today we'll build 👁 Show / Hide Password.

By the end, your Signup page will behave like Google, Microsoft, Amazon, and LinkedIn.

🎯 Step 1: Create a New State
You already have:

const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
const [confirmPassword, setConfirmpassword] = useState("");
Below them add:

const [showPassword, setShowPassword] = useState(false);
const [showConfirmPassword, setShowConfirmPassword] = useState(false);
🤔 What does false mean?
Initially,

showPassword = false
means

************
(password hidden)

When it becomes

showPassword = true
it means

React@123
(password visible)

Step 2: Change Password Input
Currently you have:

<input
    type="password"
    placeholder="Password"
    ...
/>
Replace only the type with:

type={showPassword ? "text" : "password"}
Now your input becomes:

<input
    type={showPassword ? "text" : "password"}
    placeholder="Password"
    value={password}
    ...
/>
🧠 What is this?
This is called the Ternary Operator.

It works like:

condition ? value1 : value2
Meaning:

If condition is true

↓

Take value1

Else

↓

Take value2
So

type={showPassword ? "text" : "password"}
means

If

showPassword = true
↓

type="text"
Otherwise

↓

type="password"
🎯 Step 3: Add the Eye Button
After the password input add:

<button
    type="button"
    className="toggle-password"
    onClick={() => setShowPassword(!showPassword)}
>
    {showPassword ? "🙈" : "👁"}
</button>
🤔 What does !showPassword mean?
Suppose

showPassword = false
Then

!false
becomes

true
Next click

showPassword = true
Then

!true
becomes

false
Every click switches between true and false.

🧠 React Flow
Click 👁

↓

showPassword

false

↓

true

↓

Input type changes

↓

text

↓

Password becomes visible
Click again

true

↓

false

↓

password

↓

*********
Step 4: Do the Same for Confirm Password
Exactly the same idea.

Use

type={showConfirmPassword ? "text" : "password"}
and

onClick={() =>
setShowConfirmPassword(!showConfirmPassword)
}
Step 5: CSS
For now, keep it simple.

.toggle-password{
    margin-top:8px;
    background:none;
    border:none;
    cursor:pointer;
    font-size:18px;
}
