🎯 Small Challenge
Before writing the code, answer this:

Why did we add:

.password-input{
    position: relative;
}
Choose one:

A. To make the password field responsive.

B. To give the absolutely positioned eye button a reference container.

C. To change the password into text.

Think about it first, then implement it. Once this is working, we'll move on to making the Login page just as polished as the Signup page.


b

🎉 100% Correct! ✅

The answer is B.

.password-input {
    position: relative;
}
creates a reference container for the eye button.

Without it, the eye icon doesn't know where to position itself.

Imagine your password field as a room:

+----------------------------------+
| Password                  👁      |
+----------------------------------+
The room is .password-input.

The eye icon says:

"Place me 14px from the right side of this room."

That's why we need:

position: relative;
This is one of the most frequently used CSS concepts in frontend development.

