📂 Step 1
Create a new component.

src/components/DashboardCard.jsx
DashboardCard.jsx
import "./DashboardCard.css";

function DashboardCard({ icon, title, description, onClick }) {

    return (

        <div className="dashboard-card" onClick={onClick}>

            <div className="card-icon">

                {icon}

            </div>

            <h2>{title}</h2>

            <p>{description}</p>

        </div>

    );

}

export default DashboardCard;
🧠 Stop Coding For 2 Minutes
This is one of the MOST IMPORTANT React concepts.

Look carefully.

function DashboardCard({

icon,

title,

description,

onClick

})
These are called

⭐ Props
Props are simply information sent from one component to another.

Think of it like ordering food.

Restaurant (DashboardCard)

↓

Customer tells

Pizza

Large

Extra Cheese
The restaurant doesn't decide.

The customer decides.

Exactly the same in React.

DashboardCard doesn't decide

Title

Icon

Description
The Dashboard decides.

🏗️ Step 2
Now open

Dashboard.jsx
Import

import DashboardCard from "../components/DashboardCard";
Then replace everything inside return with

<div className="dashboard">

    <h1>Welcome Back 👋</h1>

    <p>What would you like to do today?</p>

    <div className="dashboard-grid">

        <DashboardCard

            icon="➕"

            title="Create Capsule"

            description="Store a memory for future."

        />

        <DashboardCard

            icon="📦"

            title="My Capsules"

            description="View your saved memories."

        />

        <DashboardCard

            icon="👤"

            title="Profile"

            description="Manage your account."

        />

        <DashboardCard

            icon="🚪"

            title="Logout"

            description="Securely sign out."

        />

    </div>

</div>
Notice something?

We wrote

<DashboardCard
FOUR TIMES.

But we created the component only ONCE.

That's the power of React.

📂 Step 3
Create

DashboardCard.css
Paste

.dashboard-card{

    background:white;

    border-radius:20px;

    padding:30px;

    text-align:center;

    cursor:pointer;

    transition:0.3s;

    box-shadow:0 10px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);

}

.dashboard-card:hover{

    transform:translateY(-6px);

    box-shadow:0 18px 35px rgba(108,99,255,.18);

}

.card-icon{

    font-size:42px;

    margin-bottom:15px;

}

.dashboard-card h2{

    color:#6c63ff;

}

.dashboard-card p{

    color:gray;

}
📂 Step 4
Update

dashboard.css
.dashboard{

    min-height:100vh;

    background:#f7f4ff;

    padding:40px;

}

.dashboard h1{

    color:#6c63ff;

    text-align:center;

}

.dashboard p{

    text-align:center;

    color:#666;

}

.dashboard-grid{

    margin-top:40px;

    display:grid;

    grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(260px,1fr));

    gap:25px;

}
🎉 Result
Now your Dashboard becomes fully responsive.

Desktop

+-------------+  +-------------+

Create         My Capsules

+-------------+  +-------------+

Profile        Logout
Mobile

Create

My Capsules

Profile

Logout
without writing any media queries!

🧠 New CSS Concept
This line

grid-template-columns:
repeat(auto-fit,minmax(260px,1fr));
looks scary.

But it is AMAZING.

It means

"Create as many columns as possible.

But never make them smaller than 260px."

React isn't doing this.

CSS Grid is.

This is how professional responsive dashboards are built.

